WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:04.255
- [Narrator] This is the bateleur eagle.

00:00:04.255 --> 00:00:06.922
Shrinking habitats, and
inadvertent poisoning,

00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:10.893
has caused a major drop in this
majestic bird's population.

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:13.976
(gentle piano music)

00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:19.064
Bateleurs are medium-sized birds.

00:00:19.064 --> 00:00:21.859
But they have a wingspan of over six feet.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:23.820
They have dark black feathers under their

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.725
white wings, with a red face, and legs.

00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:29.726
While endemic to most parts of Africa,

00:00:29.726 --> 00:00:31.949
they prefer the open woodland savannas,

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:33.449
or the grasslands.

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:37.704
The name Bateleur is French
for tightrope walker,

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:40.814
and references their
distinctive flight pattern.

00:00:40.814 --> 00:00:42.568
Unlike most birds, they fly low to

00:00:42.568 --> 00:00:44.915
the ground when hunting for prey.

00:00:44.915 --> 00:00:47.087
They feed on small birds and mammals,

00:00:47.087 --> 00:00:49.928
but are also carrion eaters.

00:00:49.928 --> 00:00:51.912
Unfortunately, farmers will poison dead

00:00:51.912 --> 00:00:54.195
carcasses to kill off predators,

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:58.362
and bateleurs end up ingesting
the highly toxic poisons.

00:00:58.696 --> 00:01:00.696
This the bateleur eagle.

